#|

SEE BEFORE mop-unique-generic.stk


As with mop-unique-generic, we want to have generic functions with only one method.
However, as we have seen, <unique-generic>s are not very efficient, even when tuning
the call to generic functions using the MOP. The main reason of this inefficiency is
that subclasses of <generic> plainly use the MOP (written in Scheme), whereas
<generic>s use code written in C.

Hence, the idea here consists to use <generic> and a new class <unique-method>
which, when added to a generic function will delete all its previously
existing methods.
|#

;;; First define a new class <unique-method>
(define-class <unique-method> (<method>)
  ())

;;;; The MOP says that add-method will be called when a new method is
;;;; desfined. Parameters are the geneic function and the new method.
;;;; Define a new method for <unique-method>s
;;;;
(define-method add-method! ((gf <generic>) (m <unique-method>))
  ;; Classes know the methods where they appears in their specialiers. So
  ;; before deleting the methods from the gf, we will delete them from the
  ;; classes using m.
  (%debug "==> ~S ~S" add-method-in-classes! (generic-function-methods gf))
  (for-each remove-method-in-classes! (generic-function-methods gf))
  ;; Add this method in classes
  (add-method-in-classes! m)
  (%debug "DONE")
  ;; Set the list of methods of this generic to a list containing only m
  (slot-set! gf 'methods (list m))
  m)

;;;;
;;;; We can now define the syntax define-unique-method for defining a
;;;; <unique-method>, by copying the code of define-method and ensuring that
;;;; the method class is <unique-method>.
;;;; BEWARE: don't use standard define-method after using
;;;; define-unique-method, or you'll have a mix of <method> and
;;;; <unique-method> for the generic.
(define-macro (define-unique-method name args . body)
  `(begin
     (ensure-generic-function ',name)
     (add-method! ,name ,(ensure-method name args body  '<unique-method>))
     (values (void) ',name)))

#|
Let's see performance now:

(define-unique-method foo ((a <string>)) ' string)

(define (foo-proc a)
  (unless (string? a) (error "bad string ~A" a))
'string)


stklos> (time (repeat 1_000_000 (foo "a")))
;    Elapsed time: 150.504 ms
;    Allocations: 72000000 bytes in 2000000 allocation calls (GC: 38)
stklos> (time (repeat 1_000_000 (foo-proc "a")))
;    Elapsed time: 70.178 ms
;    Allocations: 0 bytes in 0 allocation calls (GC: 0)

That's better than what we had with <unique-generic>s: we have only two allocations
by call (instead of 4) and we are only two time slower.
|#

;;;;
;;;; Everything is ready. Here we go
;;;;

;;;; define log as a generic of class <unique-generic>
(define-unique-method log ([msg <string>] value) 
  (eprintf "*** ~A: ~W~%" msg value))


;;;; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
(define (main args)
  (printf "Using <unique-method>\n")
  ;; This call will succeed
  (printf "A successful call\n")
  (log "A test" 100)
  ;; This one will not
  (with-handler (lambda (c)
                  (printf "A bad call. Message was:\n~A\n"
                          (condition-message c)))
                (log 'bad 'usage)))
